 "suggestion": {
  "title": "Suggested route",
  "createdAt": "2026-10-23",
  "basis": "Built from all 29 places and events in the planner, scheduled or not (Creamfields is offered as an option because it clashes with the surf event).",
  "intro": "A north-then-south loop: Andes near Santiago first, then the Aconcagua valley and the northern coast, the surf coast around Matanzas and Pichilemu, the Maule wine valley and coast, and back to Santiago via Rancagua. Most days have one to two hours of driving, with a few longer transfer days of three hours or more. It's only an idea; your planner stays as it is.",
  "notes": [
   {
    "kind": "conflict",
    "text": "Creamfields (Sat 14 and Sun 15 Nov, Club Hípico de Santiago) clashes with the 5-Star Surf event at Matanzas on Sat 14. If you want Creamfields: drive Matanzas → Santiago on Sun 15 (about 2.5 h), sleep in Santiago, and go on to Pichilemu on Mon 16. That costs one night in Pichilemu."
   },
   {
    "kind": "tip",
    "text": "Sewell sits inside an active copper mine and can only be visited on an organised tour. Tours leave from Rancagua (Fundación Sewell, Gamero 0400), take about 8 hours and are for ages 7 to 75, so book ahead. That's why Sewell moved from 7 Nov to the end of the trip, when you pass Rancagua anyway."
   },
   {
    "kind": "tip",
    "text": "Persa Bio Bio is only open at weekends (Sat and Sun, 09:00–18:00), so it fits Saturday 7 Nov on the way to the Cajón del Maipo."
   },
   {
    "kind": "tip",
    "text": "Mountain roads (El Yeso, Portillo, Valle Nevado) can still have snow in November. Check road status before you go, and carry chains if Carabineros ask for them."
   }
  ],
